## Proof

**Claim.** The monic polynomials $f \in \mathbb{C}[X]$ with $|f(x)|=1$ for all $|x|=1$ are exactly $f(X)=X^{n}$ for $n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$.

---

**Verification that these work.** If $f(X)=X^{n}$, then for $|x|=1$, $|f(x)|=|x|^{n}=1$. ✓

**Proof that these are the only ones.**

Let $f\in\mathbb{C}[X]$ be monic of degree $n$, with roots $\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{n}$ (counted with multiplicity):
$$f(X)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}(X-\alpha_{i}).$$

Define the **reciprocal conjugate** polynomial
$$\widetilde f(X)\;=\;\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-\overline{\alpha_{i}}\,X)\;=\;X^{n}\,\overline{f(1/\overline{X})}.$$

**Key identity on the unit circle.** For $|x|=1$ we have $\overline{x}=1/x$, so
$$\overline{f(x)}=\prod_{i=1}^{n}(\overline{x}-\overline{\alpha_{i}})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}\!\left(\tfrac{1}{x}-\overline{\alpha_{i}}\right)=x^{-n}\prod_{i=1}^{n}(1-\overline{\alpha_{i}}\,x)=x^{-n}\,\widetilde f(x).$$

Hence
$$|f(x)|^{2}=f(x)\,\overline{f(x)}=x^{-n}\,f(x)\,\widetilde f(x)\qquad(|x|=1).$$

**From the hypothesis to a polynomial identity.** The hypothesis $|f(x)|=1$ on $|x|=1$ gives
$$x^{-n}\,f(x)\,\widetilde f(x)=1\quad\text{on }|x|=1,$$
i.e.
$$f(x)\,\widetilde f(x)=x^{n}\quad\text{on }|x|=1.$$

Both sides are polynomials in $x$ (the left side is a product of two polynomials), and they agree on the infinite set $\{x:|x|=1\}$. Therefore
$$\boxed{\,f(X)\,\widetilde f(X)=X^{n}\,}\tag{$\ast$}$$
as an identity in $\mathbb{C}[X]$.

**Degree comparison.** The factor $(1-\overline{\alpha_{i}}\,X)$ has degree $1$ if $\alpha_{i}\neq 0$ and degree $0$ if $\alpha_{i}=0$. Thus
$$\deg\widetilde f = \#\{i:\alpha_{i}\neq 0\}=:k.$$
Since $\deg f=n$, the left side of $(\ast)$ has degree $n+k$, while the right side has degree $n$. Therefore $n+k=n$, giving $k=0$.

**Conclusion.** All roots $\alpha_{1},\dots,\alpha_{n}$ are zero, so
$$f(X)=\prod_{i=1}^{n}(X-0)=X^{n}.\qquad\blacksquare$$

